slide('roadmap','A six-week post-album programme',heading('Proposed programme · 17 November → 28 December','One song at a time.<br><span class="serif">A reason to return.</span>')+'''<div class="programme mt"><div class="programme-row"><b>01</b><b>17–23 Nov · clear the inventory</b><p>Label checks XMU full takes, rights and edits. Channel team reviews the album playlist and catalogue entry routes.</p></div><div class="programme-row"><b>02</b><b>24–30 Nov · a focused story</b><p>Artist/editor tests a Stone Fruit explanation or approved excerpt. Add the relevant full-song destination; record a baseline.</p></div><div class="programme-row"><b>03</b><b>1–7 Dec · the next full song</b><p>If cleared, publish one session performance plus two distinct supporting edits. If unavailable, keep existing-song support; approve a new shoot separately.</p></div><div class="programme-row"><b>04</b><b>8–14 Dec · read the response</b><p>Review the first seven days in Studio. Adjust the next edit using retention and traffic; do not choose solely on public views.</p></div><div class="programme-row"><b>05</b><b>15–21 Dec · a tour connection</b><p>London is listed for 15 Dec. Capture a song-specific moment only if artist availability, filming and permissions allow.</p></div><div class="programme-row"><b>06</b><b>22–28 Dec · sustain or refine</b><p>Give the chosen song one further reason to watch. Review the session at day 28; decide whether a second full performance merits investment.</p></div></div>'''+note('A proposed operating plan, not confirmed deliverables or a proven optimal cadence. Tour context: Partisan artist listing. Weeks 3–6 depend on asset clearance, staff availability and the first results.','INFERENCE'))
slide('next-campaign','Carry the lesson into the next campaign',heading('Next campaign · plan the second chapter first','Book the follow-through<br><span class="serif">before release week.</span>')+'''<div class="rollout-board mt"><article><span class="badge">Before launch</span><h3>Build the container.</h3><p>Prepare the album playlist at announcement. Give each single a primary asset, a story and a performance option where justified.</p></article><article><span class="badge">Release week</span><h3>Keep the coverage.</h3><p>Retain full-album availability and a focus video. Choose distinct Shorts that lead to those songs; avoid duplicate delivery.</p></article><article><span class="badge">+7–14 days</span><h3>Reserve another version.</h3><p>Pre-clear one full follow-up before the album lands. A session or different interpretation should add a reason to watch.</p></article><article><span class="badge">+28 / +42 days</span><h3>Make the next decision.</h3><p>Use matched-age results to decide whether to extend the same song, move focus or stop an expensive format.</p></article></div><div class="weekly-strip"><span><b>Public:</b> day-7 / day-28 asset views and publishing continuity</span><span><b>Studio:</b> retention, traffic paths, returning viewers and subscriber contribution</span></div><p class="scene-read">Start consistent observations at announcement. That will make the next comparison about results as well as structure.</p>'''+note('7–14 days is a proposed scheduling test, not a measured Blondshell optimum. Studio metrics require authorised channel access; the public audit does not estimate them.','INFERENCE'))
slide('method','Evidence and method',heading('How to read this audit','Evidence first.<br><span class="serif">Interpretation clearly marked.</span>')+'''<div class="grid3 mt"><article class="decision"><span class="badge">API</span><h3>What is public.</h3><p>520 unique upload rows, uncapped. Titles, dates, duration, public counters, playlists and sampled comments. All asset views are lifetime at capture.</p></article><article class="decision"><span class="badge">Watcher</span><h3>What we observed.</h3><p>22 channel snapshots from 27 Oct. Limited video histories; no launch-age history or previous campaign baseline. Repeated counters restrict pace claims.</p></article><article class="decision"><span class="badge">Inference</span><h3>What we recommend.</h3><p>Programming choices drawn from the inventory and precedents. No paid/organic split, causal uplift, unique fan count or conversion rate is inferred.</p></article></div><p class="scene-read">Coverage caveat: 520 retrieved rows versus 513 reported videos; row views total 6.29m against an 8.52m channel counter. These scopes do not reconcile. No catalogue-share claim uses the channel counter.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button analysis-tab" href="#analysis">Open supporting analysis</a><a class="button secondary" href="/blondshell/research-2026-11-17.json" target="_blank">Source rows ↗</a><a class="button secondary" href="/blondshell/calculations-2026-11-17.json" target="_blank">Calculations ↗</a></div>'''+note('Captured 17 Nov 2026, 16:00 UTC via the existing Campaign Coach APIs. Source notes and complete window inventories are available in Analysis. Release dates verified against Partisan / official Bandcamp.'))
# Analysis is a separate modal, following the Vaccines architecture.
extra=[v for v in V if not v['durationSec'] and day(v['publishedAt'])>=day('2026-05-14')]
analysis='''<section id="analysis" class="analysis-sheet" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="analysis-title" tabindex="-1" hidden><header class="analysis-toolbar"><a class="brand" href="#analysis">Blondshell · supporting evidence</a><button id="close-analysis" type="button" aria-label="Close analysis">Close ×</button></header><div class="analysis-sheet-inner analysis-content appendix"><span class="label">Research appendix · 17 November 2026</span><h2 id="analysis-title">What the audit<br><span class="serif">is built on.</span></h2>
<details open><summary>Source, scope and campaign boundaries</summary><p>API catalogue: @Blondshell, UCJ1FMy0FK0aSeXosWvGXShg, captured '''+esc(D['fetchedAt'])+'''. The uploads list returned 520 unique rows / 520 playlist IDs, uncapped. Public channel counter: 513 videos, 18,800 rounded subscribers, 8,523,384 lifetime views. Retrieved rows sum to 6,290,022: an unreconciled difference of 2,233,362. Linked/OAC, unavailable or historic content may differ in scope; no specific cause is established.</p><p>Violins release: 25 September 2026. First explicit teaser in the adopted window: Heart Has To Work So Hard on 14 May; first full single 18 May; title track / album announcement 25 June. Audit ends 17 November, day 53 after release. There are 110 playable channel uploads in this window, not 110 confirmed album promotional assets. Some Shorts concern touring, catalogue, covers or personality. Eleven full-song uploads match the 11 official album tracks; 99 surrounding Shorts form the activity layer.</p><p>If You Asked For A Picture release: 2 May 2025. What’s Fair was already on the channel on 21 August 2024; the first explicit named teaser is 12 August. Full reconstruction: 12 August 2024 → 24 June 2025, through day +53. The equal-window comparison separately uses day −130 through +53: 23 December 2024–24 June 2025 versus 18 May–17 November 2026. Both contain 184 calendar days. It deliberately excludes What’s Fair from the matched publishing window, but not from the full album inventory.</p><p><a href="https://store.partisanrecords.com/products/violins-lp" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Partisan · Violins release and tracklist</a> · <a href="https://blondshell.bandcamp.com/album/violins" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Official Bandcamp · Violins</a> · <a href="https://partisanrecords.com/moment/blondshells-sophomore-album" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Partisan · previous release</a> · <a href="https://blondshell.bandcamp.com/album/if-you-asked-for-a-picture" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Previous album tracklist</a> · <a href="https://www.partisanrecords.com/artists/blondshell" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Artist page / tour / portrait by Reese Layton</a>.</p></details>
<details><summary>Format definitions, calculations and exclusions</summary><p>Short is the existing full-catalogue duration proxy: &gt;0 and ≤62 seconds. This does not establish vertical aspect ratio, native Shorts status or Shorts-feed consumption, and can miss longer Shorts. All 99 current-window Shorts use that proxy. Full-song formats were reviewed by title and description opening, against the official tracklist. Music videos: Violins and Stone Fruit. Visualizer: Heart Has To Work So Hard. Lyric: Fur Elise. Seven album-day official audios. The short announcing Fur Elise calls it a visualizer; the primary upload and Partisan label it a lyric video, which is the classification used here.</p><p>Playable current-window totals: 110 uploads, 675,544 lifetime views. Album full songs: 11, 396,051 views (58.62697% of window-row views). Surrounding Shorts: 99, 279,493 views (90% of uploads; median 2,321). Music videos: 236,044 (59.599% of album-video views); visualizer 98,977; lyric 33,902; audios 27,128. Percentages describe the captured cohorts and can change as videos age. They are not age-independent performance benchmarks.</p><p>71 uploads before release day; 15 on release day (8 full songs + 7 Shorts); 24 after release day (all Shorts). Release-week window 25 Sep–1 Oct: 18 playable uploads (8 full songs + 10 Shorts). None of these present lifetime asset counters as views generated during release week.</p><p>One Live Stream row, oHAC9CnFgmE, has duration 0, views 0 and no actualStart or scheduledStart. It is retained in raw evidence and excluded from playable upload counts. It is not evidence of a completed stream or Premiere. The catalogue is complete as returned by the API, not a census of partner/fan/Topic viewing.</p><p>Active-week counts use seven-day bins anchored to each matched start, with a final two-day bin. Longest gap is the calendar-day difference between consecutive upload dates, not a count of empty dates. Cross-album structural comparison only: current 108 uploads / 97 Shorts / 11 full-length / 23 of 27 active bins / maximum 16-day gap; previous 110 / 98 / 12 / 25 of 27 / 14 days. Previous includes the Diet Pepsi cover, so neither campaign has a new full-song album version at days +1–53.</p></details>
<details><summary>Historical snapshots and momentum restrictions</summary><p>Watcher handle mapping already resolves to the correct channel ID; no mapping mutation was needed. Channel observations run 27 Oct–17 Nov: 8,339,175 → 8,523,384 (+184,209). The same counter repeats on several dates, including 9–17 Nov. Latest API capture matches that stored value. No zero-growth week or fresh daily rate is claimed. Public subs in the returned recent series move 18,700 → 18,800 between 6–7 Nov; older subscriber values are not supplied by the all-views history.</p><p>Video-series was queried for eleven catalogue/campaign leaders. Only Fur Elise and Stone Fruit returned history. Fur Elise: 31,089 → 32,342, 27 Oct–6 Nov, 11 observations; it stops before capture. Stone Fruit: 92,168 → 97,384, 27 Oct–17 Nov, 22 observations, +5,216 across 21 elapsed days. Fresh catalogue API Stone Fruit total: 97,490. These are separate capture times and never spliced into daily increments. No series supports first-7/28-day results or previous-campaign age matching.</p></details>
<details><summary>Audience, playlists and recommendations: what is and is not established</summary><p>Seven videos scanned at relevance order, max 40 comments/video: 271 top-level comments returned; none reported disabled. Sample includes Violins, Heart Has To Work So Hard, Fur Elise, Stone Fruit, T&amp;A, 23’s A Baby and Docket live. Selected quotations are texture, not representative sentiment, a demand forecast or a paid/organic source. No ad spend or traffic split is inferred, including where individual comments mention ads or radio.</p><p>Channel-shelf returned five public playlists, including Violins (11 items, created 25 Sep 2026). Nine homepage sections: three single-playlist and six undefined. The endpoint reports counts, not the playlists pinned in each section; no exact order is asserted. No public live-specific playlist was returned. End screens, cards, Related Videos, Community and trailer performance are unobserved. The proposed housekeeping is a review, not an assertion that those features are missing.</p><p>The session Shorts establish excerpts exist. Full takes, label rights, artist permission and broadcaster usage terms are unknown. New shoots, edits, Community activity and revised playlists need the relevant owners. Proposed 7–14-day follow-ups and a six-week programme are scheduling tests, not measured optimal timings or confirmed budgets. Public output and matched-age counters should be paired with Studio retention, traffic, returning viewers and subscriber contribution when access is available.</p></details>
<details><summary>Confirmed Violins full-song inventory · all 11 tracks</summary>'''+table(L)+'''</details><details><summary>Complete current rollout window · 110 playable uploads + excluded record</summary>'''+table(C+extra)+'''</details><details><summary>Previous album: complete lead-in and first 53 days · 137 uploads</summary>'''+table(FULL)+'''</details><details><summary>Catalogue: every retrieved full-length upload</summary>'''+table(sorted([v for v in V if not v['isShort'] and v['durationSec']],key=lambda v:v['publishedAt']))+'''</details><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/blondshell/research-2026-11-17.json" target="_blank">Captured source rows ↗</a><a class="button secondary" href="/blondshell/calculations-2026-11-17.json" target="_blank">Exact calculations ↗</a></div></div></section>'''
